    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to determine the probability of two events happening together, which is known as the \"joint probability.\" We will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the probabilities of unplanned events**:\n    - The probability it will rain on Saturday is .6, or 60%.\n    - The probability it will rain on Sunday is .25, or 25%.\n\n2. **Determine the probability of both events happening together**:\n    - For events to occur simultaneously, we must assume that the events are independent. This means the probability of both events happening is simply the product of the probabilities of each event individually.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability**:\n    - The probability of it raining on both days is:\n    \\[\n    P(\\text{Rain on Saturday}) \\times P(\\text{Rain on Sunday}) = 0.6 \\times 0.25\n    \\]\n\n4. **Transfer the fraction multiplied to a percent value**:\n    - First, calculate the multiplication:\n    \\[\n    0.6 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n    \\]\n    - Convert the result from the decimal form to a percentage by multiplying by 100:\n    \\[\n    0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\%\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, the combined probability that it will rain on both days is $\\boxed{15\\%}$.",
